# combo_spec: D1/3+D8/0
# Name: 多色连接器 + 颜色编码优先级

# Design analysis for this combo variant
# -------------------------------------
# concretization and behavior trace:
# - Keep the original output-first jigsaw template: one fixed yellow anchor plus 4-6 scattered pieces.
# - Connector pixels now use exactly three visible colors {red=2, blue=1, green=3}. Same-color overlap is
#   still the only legal connection, but color also carries an ordering role.
# - The ordering role is local to each multi-port piece: when a piece contains several connector colors,
#   its highest-priority visible connector (red before blue before green) is the side that must be used to
#   attach that piece into the current assembly; lower-priority colors on that same piece are deferred ports
#   that only become available after the piece has been placed.
# - Small trace:
#   original: a red-red match is enough; after the first attachment, any remaining red port may be used next.
#   combo: suppose anchor still has one red opening, while a newly placed red-blue piece exposes a blue port.
#   A color-only solver could try the blue-green child immediately. The combo solver must not: the remaining
#   red phase has priority, and the red-blue piece itself had to enter through red rather than blue.
#
# complexity calibration:
# - Restrict connector colors to three frequent, highly salient ARC colors: red, blue, green.
# - Body colors exclude connector colors and yellow, so the dual color semantics stay readable.
# - The staged generator guarantees repeated evidence: at least one red-blue piece and at least one blue-green
#   piece exist in every sample, so examples visibly demonstrate both same-color matching and phase ordering.
# - Piece counts stay within the original range, and the tree depth is capped at three color stages to avoid
